  {
   "id": "intro",
   "type": "intro",
   "heading": {
    "en": "Doing it again"
   },
   "hook": {
    "en": "Print 1 to 100, add the digits of a number, check for a prime: each needs a step repeated. Java has three loops, and choosing the right one is part of the skill."
   },
   "objectives": [
    {
     "en": "Write for, while and do-while loops"
    },
    {
     "en": "Distinguish entry-controlled and exit-controlled loops"
    },
    {
     "en": "Use break and continue, and trace nested loops"
    }
   ],
   "narration": {
    "en": "Print 1 to 100, add the digits of a number, check for a prime: each needs a step repeated. Java has three loops, and choosing the right one is part of the skill."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "concept1",
   "type": "concept",
   "title": {
    "en": "Three loops"
   },
   "body": {
    "en": "for (init; test; update) { body } is used when the number of iterations is known. while (test) { body } tests first, so the body may run zero times. do { body } while (test); tests after the body, so the body always runs at least once. for and while are entry-controlled; do-while is exit-controlled. A loop whose test never becomes false is an infinite loop: for(;;) or while(true)."
   },
   "points": [
    {
     "en": "for: known count"
    },
    {
     "en": "while: test first, may run 0 times"
    },
    {
     "en": "do-while: runs at least once"
    },
    {
     "en": "Infinite: for(;;), while(true)"
    }
   ],
   "narration": {
    "en": "for (init; test; update) { body } is used when the number of iterations is known. while (test) { body } tests first, so the body may run zero times. do { body } while (test); tests after the body, so the body always runs at least once. for and while are entry-controlled; do-while is exit-controlled. A loop whose test never becomes false is an infinite loop: for(;;) or while(true)."
   }
  },
